# level1.script - boot, start mission 01, and play it.
#
# Run with `make recomp-smoke`. Every action goes in through the same host
# state the real keyboard and mouse feed, so this exercises the paths the game
# actually uses rather than a back door built for testing.
#
# HOW THE INPUT HAS TO ARRIVE, WHICH TOOK TWO FIXES TO GET RIGHT
#
# The front end reads DirectInput buffered data when it is told the state
# changed; it does not poll. Buffered events are differences, so:
#
#   * A move must carry a relative delta. A move reporting only a destination
#     produces no DIMOFS_X or DIMOFS_Y event and the game's own cursor never
#     moves.
#   * The game integrates those deltas into a pointer of its own that started
#     wherever it chose, so an absolute coordinate here is only a guess about
#     where that pointer already was. `moveby` sends raw relative motion, and a
#     delta larger than the screen pins the game's pointer to the top-left
#     corner. That corner is the one position a script can be sure of, and
#     every coordinate below is measured from it.
#   * A click must stay down long enough for the guest to look. Pressing and
#     releasing in one host turn leaves the button at the level it started
#     from, the shim's comparison finds no change, and both events are lost.
#     The runner holds it for 120 ms.
#
# WHERE THE COORDINATES COME FROM
#
#   * New Game at (320, 140) and the tutorial prompt's No at (320, 219): the
#     recovered menu layout in docs/MENU.md (005d9660 / 004c3290 put the first
#     action row at y 140, rows 30 apart) and docs/MENU_NEW_GAME.md (prompt top
#     159, Yes at the paragraph bottom, No 30 lower), both centred on x 320.
#   * The start control at (605, 448) and the level-01 world at (322, 235):
#     measured off a dump of the level-select screen, which is a solar system
#     with a wooden panel across the bottom. The panel's right-hand button is
#     what starts the mission; clicking the world itself does nothing.
#   * The dialogue's OK at (236, 148) and the people at (390, 212): measured off
#     a dump of the running level.

# WAIT FOR THE GAME, NOT FOR THE CLOCK
#
# This used to be `wait 16000`, and that is what made the run fail about one
# time in two. The front end is roughly eight seconds of loading, then the
# copyright screen for five, then a cross-fade into the menu, which a live
# trace put at 13.9 s - so a fixed 16 s left two seconds of margin, and every
# second added to startup since (the mod loader, Lua, the MIDI SoundFont) spent
# some of it. A click that lands before the menu is live dismisses whatever is
# on screen instead of choosing New Game, the run never leaves the front end,
# and the eight assertions about the level all fail at once with no texture and
# no 3D scene to show for it.
#
# WHY THE CLAIM HAS TO HOLD
#
# `picture` is the share of the last presented frame that is not black. The
# copyright screen settles at 0.960 - its black border and the white Bullfrog
# box - and the menu at 0.998, so 0.98 tells the two apart. But both screens
# FADE in, and a fade passes through every value on its way up: the copyright
# screen's own fade-in crosses 0.98 for a frame or two before settling below
# it. A bare `await picture>0.98` is answered there, five seconds early, and
# the run fails exactly as the fixed wait did - that is not a guess, it is what
# the first two versions of this fix did. `for 2000` requires the claim to
# stay true, which no fade does.
await picture>0.98 for 2000 within 60000
dump menu

# New Game, then answer No to the tutorial prompt.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 140
wait 700
click left 320 140
wait 3000
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 219
wait 700
click left 320 219
wait 12000
dump levelselect

# Start mission 01 from the panel's button.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 300
moveby 605 448
wait 900
click left 605 448
wait 8000
dump level_start

# Dismiss the opening dialogue.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 300
moveby 236 148
wait 700
click left 236 148
wait 4000
dump level_playing

# M1's first actions: select one of the blue braves, then order it somewhere,
# and check both against the game's own record rather than against pixels.
#
# WHY NOT PIXELS
#
# The level opens in a camera flyby, and while it runs every frame differs from
# the last whatever the player does - so "the picture changed" cannot tell a
# selected brave from an ignored click. The entity table can. It is 2000
# records of 179 bytes at 0x8e0428, laid out as games/populous/tests/entity_codec.hpp encodes
# it: flags at +12, kind at +42, state at +44, owner at +47, position at +61 as
# x, z and altitude words. A blue brave is owner 0, kind 1.
#
# THE FLYBY
#
# This waits a fixed 20 seconds for it, and says so rather than pretending to
# detect it: there is no camera-settle signal exposed to the host, and a
# frame-difference test cannot find one because the units keep animating after
# the camera stops.
wait 34000
dump flyby_over
quit
